# Untitled

Cation-induced kinetic trapping and enhanced hydrogen adsorption in a modulated anionic metal–organic framework
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs)—microporous materials constructed by bridging metal centres with organic ligands—show promise for applications in hydrogen storage, which is a key challenge in the development of the 'hydrogen economy'. Their adsorption capacities, however, have remained insufficient for practical applications, and thus strategies to enhance hydrogen–MOF interactions are required. Here we describe an anionic MOF material built from \(\ln(\mathrm{III})\) centres and tetracarboxylic acid ligands \((\mathsf{H}_{4}\mathsf{L})\) in which kinetic trapping behaviour—where hydrogen is adsorbed at high pressures but not released immediately on lowering the pressure—is modulated by guest cations. With piperazinium dications in its pores, the framework exhibits hysteretic hydrogen adsorption. On exchange of these dications with lithium cations, no hysteresis is seen, but instead there is an enhanced adsorption capacity coupled to an increase in the isosteric heat of adsorption. This is rationalized by the different locations of the cations within the pores, determined with precision by X-ray crystallography.
The widespread use and application of hydrogen as a clean alternative to fossil fuels is limited by the lack of a convenient, safe and cheap storage system<sup>1</sup>. Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) with high internal surface areas are currently being intensively studied for the storage of hydrogen<sup>2–4</sup>, and although sorption of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) by these materials often displays excellent reversibility and fast kinetics, the weak dispersive interactions that hold \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) within the framework require low operating temperatures (often 77 K) and high pressures (up to 90 bar) to achieve the 2010 US Department of Energy storage target of \(6.0\;\mathrm{wt\%}\) (http://www.eere.energy.gov/hydrogenandfuelcells/pdfs/freedomcar_targets_explanations.pdf). These target uptakes are for whole systems rather than for the storage medium alone.
Increasing the interaction between \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) molecules and the host framework, and hence the associated isosteric heat of adsorption, represents a major challenge if these systems are to find practical use at closer to ambient temperatures<sup>5</sup>. Several strategies are being explored to enhance the interaction between \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) and host frameworks. Narrow pores can provide greater overlap of potential energy fields of pore walls, thereby increasing the heat of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) adsorption at low pressures compared with larger pores<sup>6–8</sup>. However, the trade-off between strong binding energy and high storage capacity appears to limit this strategy. Neutron diffraction experiments suggest that vacant metal coordination sites within the framework can provide stronger \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) binding sites than the organic linkers alone, but the interaction between transition metals and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) molecules is still insufficient to raise the storage temperature significantly<sup>9</sup>. Furthermore, these sites may saturate very quickly at low partial pressures, thus not necessarily enhancing the overall storage capacity of the material<sup>10</sup>.
A particularly attractive approach to enhancing the capacity of MOFs to store \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) is to exploit a kinetic trap within a chemically modified porous host. This potentially enhances gas uptake by
confining the substrate \(\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\right)\) within the porous material at high pressure and not allowing it to be immediately released upon lowering the pressure, thus facilitating \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) storage at lower pressures<sup>11</sup>. Examples of hysteretic \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) adsorption by MOFs have been found in materials that undergo structural changes upon pressurization with the guest gas substrate<sup>11–14</sup>. In particular, the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) adsorption reported by Long et al.<sup>12</sup> shows temperature-dependent hysteresis loops, with a gating mechanism leading to hysteresis at 77 and \(87\;\mathrm{K}\) and disappearance of the hysteresis loop at \(50\;\mathrm{K}\). These examples rely on the framework undergoing some structural modification to allow gas uptake. In contrast, the example reported herein exhibits cation-dependent kinetic trapping of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\), where the cation is itself a guest of the framework system, and this represents a new class of flexible, modifiable MOF capable of exhibiting hysteretic adsorption. Here we demonstrate that variation of the cation, from piperazinium \((\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{ppz}^{2+})\) to \(\mathrm{Li}^{+}\), leads to loss of this hysteretic adsorption behaviour, giving rise to a strategy that may be used to design frameworks that can act as kinetic traps for \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\).
Loading of \(\mathrm{Li}(0)/\mathrm{Li}^{+}\)into MOFs has received considerable attention owing to the potential for \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) to strongly bind on free \(\mathrm{Li}\) sites<sup>15,16</sup>. This has been supported by recent computational and theoretical studies that have modelled \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) molecules adsorbed onto \(\mathrm{Li}(0)/\mathrm{Li}^{+}\)-doped MOFs and three-dimensional covalent organic framework hosts<sup>17–23</sup>. However, doping of \(\mathrm{Li}(0)\) into a real coordination framework material is intrinsically problematic owing to the reactivity of \(\mathrm{Li}(0)\) with the cationic metal clusters and organic ligands that constitute the coordination framework. Thus, doping with ionic \(\mathrm{Li}^{+}\)appears to be a more controllable route to interrogate and understand \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) binding in a doped host.
We report herein the synthesis, structure and exchange reactions of two salts \(\{[\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{ppz}][\mathrm{In}_{2}(\mathrm{L})_{2}]{\cdot}3.\mathrm{DMF}{\cdot}5\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\}_{\infty}\), 1-ppz-solv (NOTT-200), and \(\{\mathrm{Li}_{1.5}[\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{O}]_{0.5}[\mathrm{In}_{2}(\mathrm{L})_{2}]{\cdot}11\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\}_{\infty}\). 1-Li-solv (NOTT-201) of a doubly interpenetrated anionic framework,
\([\mathrm{In}_{2}(\mathrm{L})_{2}]^{2-}\), 1, constructed from \(1,1',4',1',4',1'''\)-quaterphenyl\(3,5,3'''',5'''\)-tetracarboxylate \((\mathrm{L}^{4-})\) and \(\mathrm{In}(\mathrm{III})\), together with the gas \((\mathrm{N}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) ) adsorption properties of their desolvated derivatives, 1-ppz and 1-Li, respectively. Notably, 1-ppz shows a significant kinetic trap (hysteresis) for \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) adsorption and release, whereas 1-Li shows an increase in both pore volume and, significantly, a higher isosteric heat of adsorption for \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) compared with 1-ppz. The structures of both 1-ppz-solv and 1-Li-solv have been determined unambiguously and we can therefore define the precise structural and chemical features of these materials that underpin the observed \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) uptake capacities and hysteresis. The material 1-Li-solv represents the first modulated MOF designed to anchor \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) via two chelate carboxylate groups, leaving accessible and exposed \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) sites after desolvation by mild heating.
## Results and discussion
Structural analysis and cation exchange. A solvothermal reaction of \(\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{L}\) with \(\mathrm{In}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}\) in an acidic \(\left(\mathrm{HNO}_{3}\right)\) mixture of \(\mathrm{DMF/CH}_{3}\mathrm{CN}\) at \(90\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) in the presence of piperazine \((\mathrm{C}_{4}\mathrm{H}_{10}\mathrm{N}_{2}\), ppz) affords the solvated framework complex \(\{[\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{ppz}][\mathrm{In}_{2}(\mathrm{L})_{2}]\cdot3.5\mathrm{DMF}\cdot5\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\}_{\infty}\), 1-ppz-solv, (NOTT-200). The structure of the molecule \(\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{L}\), which has an approximate length of \(17.6\,\mathrm{\AA}\) and a width of \(7.1\,\mathrm{\AA}\), is shown below.
The three pores host different guest molecules. Channel A is occupied by free solvent molecules DMF and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\), whereas channel B contains only the ordered \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{ppz}^{2+}\) dications. Channel C is empty, because it is too narrow to allow solvent molecules to enter. Two uncoordinated carboxylate oxygen atoms located at
the corners of channel B are involved in \(\mathrm{O}^{\cdot}\cdot\mathrm{H}-\mathrm{N}\) hydrogen bonds with the \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}^{+}\) groups of the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{ppz}^{2+}\) dications, \(\mathrm{N}^{\cdot}\cdot\mathrm{O}=\) 2.88(3) Å, \(\mathrm{N-H}^{\cdot}\cdot\mathrm{O}=\overline{{175^{\circ}}}\). Thus, the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{ppz}^{2+}\) dications are fully ordered and positioned in the centre of channel B, partially blocking it, whereas the free solvent molecules DMF and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) in channels A are, as expected, highly disordered. The volume fraction occupied by the guest solvent molecules, \(\mathrm{DMF}/\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\), in 1-ppz-solv was estimated by PLATON/SOLV (ref. 24) to be \(35\%\) using a default probe size of \(1.2\,\mathrm{\AA}\) (Fig. 2).
To establish the influence of the cationic species on the adsorption properties, \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{ppz}^{2+}\) dications were replaced by \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) ions via ion exchange under mild conditions to give \([\mathrm{Li}_{1.5}[\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{O}]_{0.5}[\mathrm{In}_{2}(\mathrm{L})_{2}]\cdot11\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\}_{\infty}\), 1-Li-solv, (NOTT-201). To achieve this, crystals of as-synthesized 1-ppz-solv were immersed
in a saturated solution of LiCl in deionized water/acetone \((\mathrm{v/v}\,{=}\,1/1)\) at room temperature. The crystals were soaked for ten days, during which time the LiCl solution was refreshed three times daily. After decanting the metal chloride solutions, the cation-exchanged crystals of 1-Li-solv were rinsed and soaked in distilled water/acetone \((\mathrm{v/v}\,{=}\,1/1)\) for up to three days to remove residual LiCl from the framework. Elemental analysis of a bulk sample of 1-Li-solv confirmed that no \(_\mathrm{N}\) is present in this material, and X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy confirms that no residual C remains in the sample.
The single crystal X-ray structure of 1-Li-solv shows a similar topology to that of 1-ppy-solv, namely a doubly interpenetrated 4,4-connected diamond-type framework. The crystal structure of 1-Li-solv confirms that all the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{ppz}^{2+}\) dications have indeed been removed from channel B, consistent with the results from elemental analysis, and, significantly, the precise crystallographic location of the \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) cations in 1-Li-solv within the framework structure is well-defined. The \(\mathrm{In}(\mathrm{III})\) centre in 1-Li-solv can be regarded as eight-coordinate with \(\mathrm{In-O}\) bond lengths to the carboxylate groups ranging from 2.152(5)–2.467(6) Å. Interestingly, O6 in channel B, which lies 2.805(12) Å from the \(\mathrm{In}(\mathrm{III})\) centre and is hydrogen-bonded to the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{ppz}^{2+}\) dications in 1-ppy-solv, is now bound to \(\mathrm{In}(\mathrm{III})\) in 1-Li-solv \([\mathrm{In-O}=2.336(6)\,\mathring{\mathrm{A}}]\) . The oxygen centre O7 in channel C, which is coordinated to \(\mathrm{In}(\mathrm{III})\) only in 1-ppy-solv \([\mathrm{In}-\) \(\mathrm{O}=2.292(8)\mathrm{~\AA~}]\) , is now coordinated at long range in 1-Li-solv \([\mathrm{In-O}7=2.467(6)\mathrm{~\AA}]\) (Fig. 1a). Two distinct water molecules (O9 and O10) are well located within channel C, and these hydrogen bond to O7 via \(\mathrm{O-H\cdot\cdotO}\) interactions, where \(\mathrm{O\cdot\cdotO}=2.798(15)\) , 2.934(15) Å. These two water molecules and two coordinated tetrahedral atoms (O2 and O3) from carboxylate groups constitute a tetrahedral environment for \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) to form a tetrahedral \(\mathrm{LiO}_{4}\) moiety within channel C, with the \(\mathrm{Li-O}\) distance ranging from 1.84(4) to 2.07(3) Å, and an Li–In distance of 3.19(3) Å (Fig. 1b)25. Thus, on going from 1-ppy-solv to 1-Li-solv, the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{ppz}^{2+}\) dications in channel B are replaced by \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) cations located within channel C, thereby modulating the potential porosity of the framework materials by freeing channel B via coordinative reorganization of the cationic guests.
In situ infrared spectroscopy of 1-Li-solv confirms that the water molecules coordinated to \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) can be removed by heating at \(100{-}150\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) under helium flow (Fig. 1c). This is consistent with thermal gravimetric analysis (TGA) results, which indicate no further weight loss between \(120\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and \(400\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) . In fully dehydrated 1-Li, no combination of carboxylate groups is able to fully saturate the coordination sphere around the desolvated \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) cation, thus leaving the \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) centre partially exposed (Fig. 1b). Significantly, the experimentally observed crystallographic positions of \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) centres in 1-Li-solv are in good agreement with those obtained from classical valence force fields and semi-empirical quantum modelling studies on Li-doped IRMOFI, where the minimum-energy position
Nitrogen sorption behaviour and micropore analysis. An acetone-exchanged sample of 1-ppy-solv, \([\mathrm{In}_{2}(\mathrm{L})_{2}(\mathrm{ppz})]\cdot\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{CH}_{3})_{2}\mathrm{CO},\) and the \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) -exchanged sample 1-Li-solv are stable on heating to \(120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) at \(10^{-10}\) bar for \(24~\mathrm{h}\) and give fully desolvated samples, 1-ppy and 1-Li, respectively. The \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) adsorption and desorption isotherms of 1-ppy at \(78\,\mathrm{K}\) show a marked hysteresis loop (Fig. 3a), whereas, in contrast, those of 1-Li show typical Type-I adsorption behaviour with full reversibility and no hysteresis (Fig. 3a). The Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) surface areas calculated within the pressure range \(P/P_{0}<0.10\) for 1-ppy and 1-Li were estimated as \(180\pm2\) and \(580\pm1\mathrm{~m}^{2}\mathrm{~g}^{-1}\) , respectively, indicating that the adsorption capacities of 1-Li is 3.2 times greater than that of 1-ppy. The accessible BET surface areas were also estimated from the single-crystal X-ray structure determination by using a Monte Carlo integration technique, where the probe \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) molecule is 'rolled' over the internal framework surface27. The diameter of the framework atoms of the 1-ppy and 1-Li were taken from the universal force field \((\mathrm{H}=2.57\,\mathring{\mathrm{A}},\) , \(\mathrm{C}=3.43\mathrm{~\AA~}\) , \(\mathrm{N}=3.26\mathrm{~\AA~}\) , \(\mathrm{O}=3.12\mathrm{~\AA~}\) , \(\mathrm{In}=3.98\mathrm{~\AA~}\) , \(\mathrm{Li}=2.18\,\mathring{\mathrm{A}})^{28}\) , and the values for the Lennard–Jones parameter, \(\sigma,\) of nitrogen was chosen as 3.798 Å (ref. 29).The BET surface areas calculated from single crystal X-ray structures of 1-ppy and 1-Li are 373 and \(643\;\mathrm{m}^{2}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) respectively, which are larger than those measured from \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) isotherms at \(78~\mathrm{K}\) . This is consistent with results obtained for IRMOF-1, 6, 9, 13 and HKUST-1, in which similar discrepancies have been attributed to the quality of the powder sample and the inherent limitations of the BET theory27. In this study, incomplete solvent removal, partial framework collapse and different pressure ranges used for BET analysis could also be responsible. Applying Dubinin–Astakhov analysis to the isotherm data confirms that the pore sizes are distributed widely around \(4.3\mathrm{~\AA~}\) for 1-ppy, and narrowly around \(8\,\mathrm{AA}\) for 1-Li, indicating that the multiple pore size distribution
in 1-ppz has been simplified in 1-Li, consistent with the replacement of bulky \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{ppz}^{2+}\) dications with smaller \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) cations. The pore volumes for 1-ppz and 1-Li calculated from the maximum \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) adsorption are 0.136 and \(0.239\;\mathrm{g}\;\mathrm{cm}^{-3}\), respectively.
The kinetic data for adsorption/desorption of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) in 1-ppz confirm the equilibrium time to be near 30 minutes, which is significantly longer than the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) equilibrium step of less than three minutes
typically observed for 1-Li and most MOFs. To investigate the mechanism of the kinetic trap of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) within 1-ppz, kinetic profiles were measured over a narrow temperature range \((80.6{-}83.9\pm\) \(0.1\;\mathrm{K})\), at which the material was expected to display similar uptake capacities. Because \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) uptake by physisorption is sensitive to temperature, the narrow range was carefully chosen to give a similar adsorption potential to the bare materials. In these experiments, samples were exposed to a pressure of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) in the range of 10–30 mbar, corresponding to \(0.5{-}1.0\ \mathrm{H}_{2}\) per indium cation, and the amount of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) adsorbed was monitored as a function of time. As shown in Fig. 4, the kinetic profile at 80.6, 81.9, 82.9 and \(83.9\;\mathrm{K}\) exhibit exponential behaviour and the data were fitted using a double-exponential expression used previously to model gas sorption kinetics in metal–organic frameworks\(^{12,30}\):
\[
\frac{M_{\mathrm{t}}}{M_{\mathrm{e}}}=A_{1}\big(1-e^{-k_{1}t}\big)+A_{2}\big(1-e^{-k_{2}t}\big)
\]
where \(M_{\mathrm{t}}\) and \(M_{\mathrm{e}}\) represent the adsorbed amount of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) at time \(t\) and at equilibrium, respectively; \(k_{1}\) and \(k_{2}\) are rate constants; and \(A_{1}\) and \(A_{2}\) are the relative contributions of two distinct barriers controlling the overall adsorption, with \(A_{1} + A_{2} = 1\).
In contrast, \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) isotherms of 1-Li show full reversibility with no hysteresis. Furthermore, the kinetic data confirm that equilibrium is achieved rapidly, within approximately 1–3 minutes of the isotherm pressure step (Fig. 3c). Importantly, the present study shows a cation-dependent hysteretic \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) sorption, with the hysteresis tunable by post-synthetic modification via cation exchange. This is distinct from previously reported hysteretic \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) sorption in flexible MOFs, based on the kinetics of pore windows opening and closing11–14.
The total \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) uptakes at 1.0 bar for 1-ppz and 1-Li are \(0.96\;\mathrm{wt}\%\) and \(1.02\;\mathrm{wt}\%\) , respectively. Although the \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) isotherms show a twofold increase in storage capacity on going from 1-ppz to desolvated 1-Li, the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) isotherms at \(78\,\mathrm{K}\) do not give such an enhancement (Fig. 3a–c). This most likely originates from the different kinetic diameters of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) \((2.89\,\mathring{\mathrm{A}})\) and \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) \((2.99\,\mathring{\mathrm{A}})\) molecules, as there are voids that are too small to incorporate \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) molecules but that can accommodate \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) . Considering the identical framework topology and pore surface chemistry in 1-ppz and 1-Li, we assume that the high energy barrier \((5{-}15\;\mathrm{kJ}\;\mathrm{mol}^{-1})\) for diffusion of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) is due to the bulky \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{pp}z^{2+}\) dications sited in channel B and the overall relatively small pore size of 1-ppz. By assuming a density of \(0.0708\;\mathrm{g\;cm}^{-3}\) of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) at its boiling point of \(20.28~\mathrm{K}\) (ref. 34), it can be deduced that the volumes of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) adsorbed in
1-ppz and 1-Li at 1.0 bar are 0.136 and \(0.144~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) , respectively. This corresponds to \(47\%\) and \(40\%\) filling of the total crystallographically determined extra-framework/cation pore volume of 0.289 and \(0.360~\mathrm{cm}^{3}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) for 1-ppz and 1-Li, respectively. At 20 bar, up to 67 and \(76\%\) of the total pore volume can be filled by \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) molecules in 1-ppz and 1-Li, respectively. These values are reasonable considering that the adsorption temperature is well above the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) critical temperature \((33\,\mathrm{K})\) and are similar to those observed in other MOF materials11.
It is also reasonable to expect increased \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) uptake for 1-Li in comparison to 1-ppz and this can be attributed to an increase in the available pore volume in the host material35. It should also be noted that the pore volumes calculated from single-crystal X-ray data are, in most cases, larger than those determined from \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) isotherms. This could be due to a number of factors. The calculated pore volumes rely on the chosen probe size, with a smaller probe size resulting in a larger apparent pore volume24. Additionally, not all the void space within a porous material is necessarily accessible by the gaseous substrate in the BET experiment, and the powder sample may not correspond precisely to the structure determined by single-crystal diffraction owing to framework collapse, residual free solvent molecules in the pores or residual reactant molecules in the pores27,36.
Hydrogen adsorption enthalpy analysis. It has been established that small pores result in strong overlapping potentials and thus high \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) adsorption heat8–10. Virial analysis31,37 of the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) adsorption isotherms measured at \(78\,\mathrm{K}\) and \(88\,\mathrm{K}\) revealed that the isosteric heat of adsorption of the small-pored 1-ppz at zero surface coverage is \(9.0\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol}^{-1}\) , which is higher than most MOFs with large pores3,4,35. However the most intriguing result is that 1-Li, even though it has a larger overall pore volume, gives a higher value of \(10.1\;\mathrm{kJ\;mol}^{-1}\) for the isosteric heat of adsorption for \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) uptake at zero surface coverage; and furthermore, it is \({\sim}1.0\;\mathrm{kJ\;mol}^{-1}\) higher than 1-ppz at all measured \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) loadings (Fig. 3d). This is very unusual, given that 1-ppz and 1-Li have the same framework structure and the same pore surface chemistry, defined by the carboxylate ligand and the \(\mathrm{In}(\mathrm{III})\) building blocks. Significantly, the smaller pore size of 1-ppz \((4.3\mathrm{~\AA~})\) does not afford a higher \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) adsorption isosteric heat than large-pored 1-Li \((8.3\mathrm{~\AA~})\) . It is reasonable, therefore, to conclude that the exposed \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) ion in 1-Li is able to provide a strong non-dissociative interaction with \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) molecules in accord with theoretical and modelling predictions17–22.
