## Conclusions
This work establishes a new protocol to modulate hysteretic \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) adsorption behaviour in metal–organic frameworks by judicious choice of cations within parent anionic frameworks. The relatively bulky \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{ppz}^{2+}\) dication hydrogen bonded within the pores gives rise to a framework that acts as a kinetic trap for \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\), with the dication acting as a reversible gate controlling entry and release of gaseous substrates. Cation exchange and incorporation of exposed \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) sites within the framework increases both the porosity and the isosteric heat of adsorption for \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\), and leads to a loss of hysteretic adsorption properties. We anticipate that the strategy developed here, based on post-synthetic ion exchange, can lead to the effective and efficient design, optimization and discovery of new exposed- \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) containing \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) storage materials with hysteretic adsorption properties operating at ambient temperatures.
## Methods
Synthesis of \(\mathbf{H}_{4}\mathbf{L}\) . \(1,1^{\prime},4^{\prime},4^{\prime\prime},4^{\prime\prime\prime},\) -quaterphenyl- \(3,5,3^{\prime\prime\prime},5^{\prime\prime\prime}\) -tetracarboxylic acid was synthesized using the literature method8. All other reagents were purchased from Sigma-Aldrich or Acros.
Syntheses of \(\{[\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{ppz}][\mathrm{In}_{2}(\mathrm{L})_{2}]\cdot3.5\mathrm{DMF}{\cdot}5\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\}_{\infty}\) (1-ppz-solv) and
\(\{\mathrm{Li}_{1.5}(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{O})_{0.5}[\mathrm{In}_{2}(\mathrm{L})_{2}]\cdot11\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\}_{\infty}\) (1-Li-solv). \(\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{L}\) \(\mathrm{0.015\;g,0.05\;mmol)}\) , \(\mathrm{In}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}\) \((0.0137\ \mathrm{g},\,0.05\ \mathrm{\mmol})\) , and piperazine \((0.015\ \mathrm{g},\,0.18\ \mathrm{mmol})\) were mixed and dispersed in a mixture of DMF/CH3CN (3 mL, \(2.1\ \mathrm{v/v}\) ). The resulting white slurry turned clear on addition of two drops of 6 m hNO3 solution. The solution was then heated to \(90~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for one day and colourless octahedral crystals of 1-ppz-solv were separated by filtration and washed sequentially by DMF, and dried in air. Yield: \(0.09\mathrm{~g~}(70\%)\) . To prepare the \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) exchanged sample, crystals of as-synthesized 1-ppz-solv were immersed in a saturated solution of LiCl in distilled water/acetone \((1{:}1\ \mathrm{v/v})\) at room temperature. The crystals were soaked for ten days, with the LiCl solution refreshed three times daily. After decanting the metal chloride solutions, the cation-exchanged crystals of 1-Li-solv were rinsed and soaked in distilled water/acetone \((1{:}1\ \mathrm{v/v})\) for three days to remove residual free LiCl. Elemental analysis \((\%\) calc./found) for 1-ppz-solv, \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4.5}\mathrm{C}_{70.5}\mathrm{H}_{24.5}\mathrm{S}_{5.5}\mathrm{C}_{66}\mathrm{H}_{41.5}\mathrm{Li}_{1.5}\) (18n; C 52.25/52.19; H 4.63/4.45; N 4.75/4.65); for 1-Li-solv, \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{5.5}\mathrm{C}_{50}\mathrm{H}_{51.5}\mathrm{Li}_{1.5}\) (15.15, 16.35/15.93; Li: 0.74/0.72; C 47.62/47.89; H 3.76/3.70; N 0.0/0.0). The volatility of crystallization solvents in the samples contributes to the discrepancy in elemental analytical data. Selected IR: for 1-ppz-solv, \(\nu=3,630(\mathrm{w})\) , 3,015(w), 2,946(w), 2,811(w), 2,324(w), 1,718(s), 1,572(m), 1,435(m), 1,365(vs), 1,251(m), 1,021(m), 918(m), 826(m), 776(s), 751(s), 663